    "description": "Model an observer-dependent geometry by assigning each observer a topology on a fixed set; the 'real' topology is the set of open sets on which all observers agree, which is the supremum in the lattice of topologies. Conjecture that the Zariski topology on the affine plane over the reals is not the agreement of any two strictly finer topologies, but is the agreement of three. Approach: prove a lower bound by showing that any two strictly finer topologies whose common open sets are exactly the Zariski opens would have to separate the zero-dimensional intersection points of algebraic curves using only two one-directional refinements, which is impossible; then exhibit an explicit three-observer representation. Contrast with the real line, whose Euclidean topology is exactly the agreement of the lower-limit and upper-limit topologies (two observers).",
