  {
    "title": "The base-(b-1) unit spectrum of digit-sharing factorisations",
    "description": "A digit-sharing factorisation is a factorisation v = x*y in which the base-b digits of x together with those of y form a permutation of the digits of v (vampire numbers are the base-10 even-length case). Because such a permutation conserves the digit sum, and a number is congruent to its digit sum modulo b-1, one obtains the multiplicative unit identity (x-1)(y-1) = 1 in the integers modulo b-1, so each factor minus one is a unit. The forward direction holds in every base b >= 2. Proposed project: prove the converse-flavoured equidistribution statement that every unit residue class modulo b-1 is realised by infinitely many digit-sharing factorisations, and study how the realised classes vary with the base. Approach: combine the conserved digit-sum congruence with constructions that append controlled digit blocks to force a prescribed residue.",
    "tags": ["NumberTheory", "Digits", "ModularArithmetic"]
  },
  {
    "title": "The mod-3 taboo as a density-1/3 sieve for vampire numbers",
