    "title": "Binary monsters: submultiplicative digit-sum floor and asymptotic count",
    "description": "Define a binary monster as a factorisation v = x*y whose binary digits of x and y jointly permute the binary digits of v. Sharing all binary digits forces s2(x) + s2(y) = s2(v), where s2 is the binary sum-of-digits. Since s2 is submultiplicative (s2(x*y) <= s2(x)*s2(y), because multiplying by a power of two only shifts bits), one gets s2(x) + s2(y) <= s2(x)*s2(y), which forbids any factor from being a power of two (s2 >= 2 for each fang). Proposed project: turn this two-sided constraint into an asymptotic count of binary monsters below 2^n, conjecturally of order 2^n / n. Approach: combine the submultiplicative floor with known distributions of s2 over dyadic blocks.",
